E.append(bs("ventral-tegmental-area", "Ventral tegmental area", "midbrain",
 "The ventral tegmental area is the group of dopamine neurons in the ventromedial midbrain tegmentum medial to the substantia nigra, above the interpeduncular nucleus and lateral to the midline, the source of the mesolimbic and mesocortical dopamine systems. Its axons run in the medial forebrain bundle to the nucleus accumbens, the amygdala, the hippocampus and the prefrontal cortex and signal reward prediction, motivation and salience; it is the substrate of addiction, of the reinforcing effects of every drug of abuse, of anhedonia and of the positive symptoms treated by dopamine antagonists. It is relatively spared in Parkinson disease but contributes to apathy and to the dopamine dysregulation syndrome, and it is damaged in Wernicke encephalopathy and paramedian midbrain infarcts.",
 "Ventromedial midbrain tegmentum on each side of the midline, medial to the substantia nigra pars compacta and the medial lemniscus, dorsal to the interpeduncular nucleus and the fossa, ventral to the red nucleus and the oculomotor nerve fibers, extending from the mammillary bodies to the rostral pons.",
 "Dopamine neurons of the ventral tegmental area burst when a reward is better than predicted and pause when it is worse, teaching the accumbens and the prefrontal cortex which cues and actions lead to reward; tonic activity sets motivation and effort. Mesocortical dopamine tunes prefrontal working memory in an inverted-U relation, and mesolimbic dopamine drives approach behavior and the incentive value of cues, which drugs of abuse hijack by raising accumbens dopamine directly or through the ventral tegmental area. GABAergic and glutamatergic neurons within the area modulate the dopamine cells and project to the same targets, and the area receives the prefrontal cortex, the lateral hypothalamus (orexin), the pedunculopontine and laterodorsal tegmental nuclei, the lateral habenula and the raphe.",
 MID + ["artery-pcom"],
 [mview("axial", "ventral-tegmental-area-l", "ventral tegmental area medial to the substantia nigra"), mview("coronal", "ventral-tegmental-area-r", "ventromedial midbrain tegmentum above the interpeduncular fossa")],
 "The paramedian ventral midbrain tegmentum between the red nuclei and the interpeduncular fossa on axial images; on neuromelanin-sensitive images it is continuous with the medial pars compacta.",
 [pathol("Paramedian midbrain infarct", "MRI", "Restricted diffusion of the ventromedial midbrain with third nerve palsy, contralateral ataxia or hemiparesis and apathy or hypersomnia.", sequence="DWI"),
  pathol("Wernicke encephalopathy", "MRI", "FLAIR hyperintensity of the periaqueductal gray and the medial midbrain tegmentum including the ventral tegmental area, with the mammillary bodies.", sequence="FLAIR"),
  pathol("Addiction and psychosis (functional)", "PET", "Increased striatal dopamine release to drugs and cues and elevated presynaptic dopamine synthesis in psychosis on PET; structural MRI normal.", sequence="n/a")],
 [("Apathy, anhedonia and loss of motivation", "bilateral", "Loss of mesolimbic dopamine reward signaling"), ("Impaired working memory and attention", "bilateral", "Loss of mesocortical dopamine to the prefrontal cortex"), ("Contribution to hypersomnia after midbrain lesions", "bilateral", "Loss of the arousal-promoting dopaminergic component of the ascending systems")],
 ["Apathy and reward responsiveness history", "Working memory and attention testing", "Substance use history, because this system is the target of every addictive drug"],
 ["The ventral tegmental area is where wanting is made; every drug of abuse raises its dopamine in the accumbens", "Antipsychotics treat delusions by blocking the receptors that mesolimbic dopamine drives, and cause parkinsonism by blocking the nigrostriatal ones", "Thiamine deficiency injures this region along with the mammillary bodies; apathy is part of Wernicke encephalopathy"],
 [R("sp-substantia-nigra"), R("sp-dopamine"), R("sp-mesencephalon-midbrain")], synonyms=["VTA", "A10 dopaminergic group"], territories=["territory-basilar", "territory-posterior-choroidal-thalamoperforating"], pathways=["pathway-basal-ganglia-loops", "pathway-papez-limbic"], syndromes=["syn-wernicke-korsakoff", "syn-parkinsonism"],
 afferents=[("Prefrontal cortex", "corticotegmental fibers"), ("Lateral hypothalamus (orexin) and pedunculopontine nucleus", "descending medial forebrain bundle and cholinergic fibers"), ("Lateral habenula (via the rostromedial tegmental nucleus)", "fasciculus retroflexus")],
 efferents=[("Nucleus accumbens and ventral striatum", "mesolimbic pathway in the medial forebrain bundle"), ("Prefrontal cortex", "mesocortical pathway"), ("Amygdala and hippocampus", "mesolimbic fibers")],
 pitfalls=["Apathy after a midbrain or thalamic stroke is often labeled depression; it lacks the sadness and reflects dopaminergic disconnection"], tags=["dopamine", "reward", "mesolimbic"]))

E.append(cortex("globus-pallidus-externus", "Globus pallidus, external segment", "x",
 "The external segment of the globus pallidus is the lateral part of the pallidum, between the putamen and the medial medullary lamina that separates it from the internal segment. It is the relay of the indirect pathway: striatal neurons carrying D2 receptors inhibit it, it in turn inhibits the subthalamic nucleus, and the subthalamic nucleus excites the output nuclei, so activity in this circuit suppresses unwanted movement. Its overactivity or underactivity shapes the balance between akinesia and chorea, and it is the origin of the ansa lenticularis fibers that surround the internal capsule; it is involved in lenticulostriate infarcts and hemorrhages and in the pallidal necrosis of carbon monoxide poisoning.",
 "Lateral segment of the lentiform nucleus medial to the putamen and the lateral medullary lamina, lateral to the internal segment and the medial medullary lamina, above the anterior commissure and the substantia innominata, medial to the external capsule and lateral to the posterior limb of the internal capsule.",
 "External pallidal neurons fire tonically and are inhibited by the striatal indirect-pathway neurons when dopamine acting on D2 receptors is low; their own inhibitory projection to the subthalamic nucleus then lets the subthalamus excite the internal pallidum and the pars reticulata, which shuts down the thalamus. Reciprocal subthalamo-pallidal connections form a pacemaker whose abnormal synchronization in the beta band underlies parkinsonian bradykinesia. Arkypallidal neurons project back to the striatum to cancel actions in progress, and prototypic neurons also reach the internal pallidum directly. Loss of striatal input to it in Huntington disease removes the brake and produces chorea.",
 ["arteries-lenticulostriate", "artery-mca", "artery-anterior-choroidal"],
 [mview("axial", "globus-pallidus-externus-l", "external pallidum between the putamen and the internal segment"), mview("coronal", "globus-pallidus-externus-r", "lateral pallidum medial to the putamen")],
 "The lateral, larger part of the pallidum on axial T2 images, darker than the putamen because of its iron and myelin, separated from the internal segment by the thin bright medial medullary lamina.",
 [pathol("Carbon monoxide poisoning", "MRI", "Bilateral symmetric restricted diffusion and later T2 hyperintensity of the globus pallidus with delayed white-matter change; parkinsonism and cognitive decline follow.", sequence="DWI"),
  pathol("Huntington disease", "MRI", "Caudate and putaminal atrophy with enlarged frontal horns; pallidal volume loss follows striatal loss and correlates with chorea giving way to rigidity.", sequence="T1"),
  pathol("Lenticulostriate infarct", "MRI", "Restricted diffusion of the lentiform nucleus involving both pallidal segments with contralateral dystonia, chorea or hemiparesis from capsular extension.", sequence="DWI")],
 [("Contralateral chorea or ballism with striatal disconnection", "contralateral", "Loss of indirect-pathway suppression through the external pallidum and subthalamic nucleus"), ("Contralateral dystonia", "contralateral", "Abnormal pallidal output patterns after lenticular injury"), ("Contribution to parkinsonian bradykinesia", "bilateral", "Excessive subthalamo-pallidal synchronization when dopamine is lost")],
 ["Observe for chorea, dystonia and bradykinesia on the side opposite a lentiform lesion", "Carboxyhemoglobin and exposure history in bilateral pallidal lesions", "Cognitive testing, because the pallidal loops include prefrontal circuits"],
 ["The external pallidum is the brake of the indirect pathway; remove its striatal input and the limbs dance", "Bilateral pallidal lesions on MRI mean carbon monoxide, cyanide, hypoxia or a metabolic disease until proved otherwise", "The beta rhythm of parkinsonism is a duet between the external pallidum and the subthalamic nucleus"],
 [R("sp-globus-pallidus"), R("sp-basal-ganglia"), R("sp-extrapyramidal-system")], synonyms=["GPe", "lateral pallidum"], system="basal-ganglia", subsystem="pallidum", parent="globus-pallidus", territories=["territory-lateral-lenticulostriate", "territory-anterior-choroidal-thalamoperforating"], pathways=["pathway-basal-ganglia-loops"], syndromes=["syn-hemiballismus", "syn-huntington-chorea", "syn-parkinsonism"],
 afferents=[("Striatum (indirect pathway, D2 neurons)", "striatopallidal fibers"), ("Subthalamic nucleus", "subthalamopallidal fibers"), ("Substantia nigra pars compacta", "dopaminergic collaterals")],
 efferents=[("Subthalamic nucleus", "pallidosubthalamic fibers"), ("Globus pallidus internus and substantia nigra pars reticulata", "direct pallidal fibers"), ("Striatum (arkypallidal neurons)", "pallidostriatal fibers")],
 pitfalls=["Pallidal T2 hypointensity increases with age; only signal change on diffusion or T2 hyperintensity indicates injury"], tags=["indirect pathway", "pallidum", "chorea"]))

E.append(cortex("globus-pallidus-internus", "Globus pallidus, internal segment", "x",
 "The internal segment of the globus pallidus is the medial, smaller part of the pallidum, against the posterior limb of the internal capsule and separated from the external segment by the medial medullary lamina. It is the main output nucleus of the basal ganglia for the limbs: its GABAergic neurons tonically inhibit the ventral anterior and ventrolateral thalamus and the pedunculopontine nucleus through the ansa lenticularis and the lenticular fasciculus, and movement is released when the direct pathway pauses them. It is the target of pallidotomy and of deep brain stimulation for Parkinson disease and dystonia, and it is damaged in kernicterus, in carbon monoxide poisoning and in the pantothenate kinase iron disorder.",
 "Medial part of the lentiform nucleus between the medial medullary lamina and the posterior limb of the internal capsule, above the optic tract and the substantia innominata, in front of and lateral to the subthalamic nucleus, divided by an accessory lamina into outer and inner portions.",
 "Internal pallidal neurons discharge continuously to keep the motor thalamus and the brainstem locomotor region suppressed; striatal direct-pathway neurons inhibit them to open a window for the intended movement while subthalamic excitation closes it around competing movements, giving the focused selection the basal ganglia provide. The output leaves through the ansa lenticularis (from the outer portion) and the lenticular fasciculus (from the inner portion), which unite as the thalamic fasciculus in the field H1 of Forel. Excess pallidal output in parkinsonism freezes the thalamus and lesioning or high-frequency stimulation of the nucleus relieves rigidity, bradykinesia and dyskinesia; abnormal patterned output produces dystonia.",
 ["arteries-lenticulostriate", "artery-anterior-choroidal"],
 [mview("axial", "globus-pallidus-internus-l", "internal pallidum against the posterior limb of the internal capsule"), mview("coronal", "globus-pallidus-internus-r", "medial pallidum above the optic tract")],
 "The medial, smaller pallidal segment on axial T2 images, separated from the external segment by the medial medullary lamina; its posteroventral part is the surgical target, planned on the AC-PC plane above the optic tract.",
 [pathol("Pallidal deep brain stimulation", "CT", "Electrodes in the posteroventral internal pallidum on postoperative CT fused with MRI for Parkinson disease with dyskinesia or for generalized dystonia.", sequence="CT non-contrast"),
  pathol("Kernicterus and pantothenate kinase-associated neurodegeneration", "MRI", "Bilateral pallidal signal change: T2 hyperintensity in kernicterus, the eye-of-the-tiger central hyperintensity within a hypointense pallidum in PKAN.", sequence="T2"),
  pathol("Anterior choroidal artery infarct", "MRI", "Restricted diffusion of the medial pallidum and posterior limb with contralateral hemiparesis, hemisensory loss and hemianopia.", sequence="DWI")],
 [("Contralateral dystonia or athetosis", "contralateral", "Abnormally patterned pallidal output to the thalamus"), ("Relief of contralateral parkinsonism and dyskinesia after lesion or stimulation", "contralateral", "Removal of excessive tonic inhibition of the motor thalamus"), ("Apathy and cognitive slowing with bilateral lesions", "bilateral", "Interruption of the prefrontal and limbic pallidal loops")],
 ["Dystonia at rest and with action; tremor and bradykinesia", "Levodopa-induced dyskinesia severity when planning surgery", "Bilirubin history in childhood dystonia"],
 ["The internal pallidum is the basal ganglia's exit door; pallidotomy props it open", "Bilateral pallidal stimulation is the best treatment for generalized dystonia", "Eye of the tiger means iron in the internal pallidum: think PKAN"],
 [R("sp-globus-pallidus"), R("sp-basal-ganglia"), R("sp-extrapyramidal-system")], synonyms=["GPi", "medial pallidum"], system="basal-ganglia", subsystem="pallidum", parent="globus-pallidus", territories=["territory-anterior-choroidal-thalamoperforating", "territory-lateral-lenticulostriate"], pathways=["pathway-basal-ganglia-loops"], syndromes=["syn-parkinsonism", "syn-anterior-choroidal-infarct", "syn-hemiballismus"],
 afferents=[("Striatum (direct pathway, D1 neurons)", "striatopallidal fibers"), ("Subthalamic nucleus", "subthalamopallidal fibers"), ("Globus pallidus externus", "pallidopallidal fibers")],
 efferents=[("Ventral anterior and ventrolateral thalamus", "ansa lenticularis and lenticular fasciculus (thalamic fasciculus)"), ("Pedunculopontine nucleus", "pallidotegmental fibers"), ("Centromedian-parafascicular thalamus", "pallidothalamic fibers")],
 pitfalls=["Pallidal stimulation can worsen gait and speech; the target treats the limbs better than the axis"], tags=["basal ganglia output", "DBS", "dystonia"]))

E.append(cortex("ventricle-lateral-inferior-horn", "Lateral ventricle, inferior (temporal) horn", "x",
 "The inferior horn of the lateral ventricle curves forward and downward from the atrium into the medial temporal lobe, ending behind the amygdala, with the hippocampus bulging into its floor and the tail of the caudate and the stria terminalis in its roof. It is the ventricular space that dilates first in obstructive hydrocephalus and in the trapped-ventricle syndrome, that widens with hippocampal atrophy in Alzheimer disease and mesial temporal sclerosis, and whose choroid plexus and fissure are the site of choroidal fissure cysts; its relation to the hippocampus makes it the approach corridor for temporal lobe surgery, and its dilatation on the opposite side is a sign of uncal herniation.",
 "Temporal horn of the lateral ventricle within the medial temporal lobe, running from the collateral trigone forward and inferiorly to a blind end just behind the amygdala; floor formed by the hippocampus and the collateral eminence, roof by the tapetum of the corpus callosum, the caudate tail and the stria terminalis, medial wall by the choroid fissure and the fimbria.",
 "The inferior horn is part of the CSF pathway from the choroid plexus of the lateral ventricle to the foramen of Monro, though its own plexus (continuous with that of the atrium through the choroidal fissure) contributes to CSF production; its size reflects the volume of the surrounding medial temporal structures and the pressure in the ventricular system. It is bounded by the structures of memory, so its enlargement is a marker of hippocampal loss, and it lies next to the optic radiation in its lateral wall, which is why temporal horn surgery risks a superior quadrantanopia. Its anterior tip is the landmark for the amygdala on coronal images.",
 ["artery-anterior-choroidal", "artery-pca"],
 [mview("coronal", "ventricle-lateral-inferior-horn-l", "temporal horn above the hippocampus"), mview("axial", "ventricle-lateral-inferior-horn-r", "inferior horn curving into the medial temporal lobe")],
 "A thin CSF-signal slit above the hippocampus on coronal images, often barely visible in young adults; the choroid plexus within it enhances normally. Its anterior tip lies just behind the amygdala.",
 [pathol("Obstructive hydrocephalus", "CT", "Rounding and dilatation of the temporal horns is the earliest and most sensitive sign of raised ventricular pressure, preceding frontal horn enlargement.", sequence="CT non-contrast"),
  pathol("Mesial temporal sclerosis and Alzheimer disease", "MRI", "Asymmetric or bilateral widening of the temporal horn with hippocampal atrophy; rated with the medial temporal atrophy scale.", sequence="T1"),
  pathol("Trapped temporal horn", "MRI", "Isolated dilatation of one temporal horn from obstruction at the atrium by tumor, hemorrhage or adhesions, causing mass effect and herniation.", sequence="T2"),
  pathol("Choroidal fissure cyst", "MRI", "Small CSF-signal cyst in the choroidal fissure medial to the temporal horn, a normal variant that must not be called a tumor or a dilated horn.", sequence="T2")],
 [("Sign of raised intracranial pressure or hydrocephalus", "bilateral", "Dilatation of the temporal horns with obstruction of CSF flow"), ("Marker of hippocampal atrophy", "ipsilateral", "Ex vacuo widening as the medial temporal lobe shrinks"), ("Superior quadrantanopia after surgery or with lateral-wall lesions", "contralateral", "Injury to the optic radiation in the lateral wall of the horn")],
 ["Measure temporal horn width on every CT read for hydrocephalus", "Grade medial temporal atrophy on coronal T1 in cognitive complaints", "Visual fields after temporal lobe surgery"],
 ["Round temporal horns on CT mean hydrocephalus; slit-like ones mean it has been treated or never was", "The temporal horn widens as the hippocampus shrinks; the two are measured together", "A trapped temporal horn is a surgical emergency that looks like a cyst"],
 [R("sp-hippocampus"), R("sp-temporal-lobe"), R("sp-hydrocephalus")], synonyms=["temporal horn", "cornu inferius"], system="ventricles-csf", subsystem="lateral", parent="ventricle-lateral", territories=["territory-anterior-choroidal-thalamoperforating"], pathways=["pathway-visual"], syndromes=["syn-hydrocephalus", "syn-uncal-herniation", "syn-normal-pressure-hydrocephalus"],
 afferents=[("Choroid plexus of the atrium and inferior horn", "CSF production"), ("Body of the lateral ventricle", "CSF flow via the atrium")],
 efferents=[("Atrium and body of the lateral ventricle toward the foramen of Monro", "CSF flow")],
 pitfalls=["Asymmetry of the temporal horns is common in normal brains; it is the combination with hippocampal signal or size change that matters"], tags=["ventricle", "hydrocephalus", "temporal horn"]))

write(E)
